# Docker Containers, PID 1, Foreground Processes, and systemd Notes

============================================================
1. Why systemctl does not work inside most containers
============================================================

Most Docker containers do NOT run systemd.

Normal Linux machine:
    PID 1 = systemd

Most containers:
    PID 1 = app process / shell / entrypoint

systemctl talks to systemd over DBus.
If systemd is absent:

    systemctl status ssh

returns:

    System has not been booted with systemd as init system

Inside containers use:
    ps
    service
    ss
instead of systemctl.

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
2. Understanding: ps -p 1 -f
============================================================

Command:
    ps -p 1 -f

Meaning:
    ps      = show processes
    -p 1    = show only PID 1
    -f      = full details

PID 1 is VERY important because:
    - it is the first process started
    - Docker tracks container lifecycle through PID 1

If PID 1 exits:
    -> container stops

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
3. Why tail -f /dev/null is used
============================================================

Example:

    /bin/sh -c "
        service ssh restart &&
        service cron restart &&
        tail -f /dev/null
    "

What happens:
    1. start ssh
    2. start cron
    3. keep shell alive forever

Why needed:
    Containers stop when PID 1 exits.

Without tail:
    service ssh restart
    -> command finishes
    -> shell exits
    -> container exits

tail -f /dev/null means:
    "do nothing forever"

It acts as a fake foreground process.

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
4. Difference between foreground in Linux vs Docker
============================================================

NORMAL LINUX TERMINAL:
    foreground = process attached to terminal

Example:
    bash
      -> apache2

Your shell becomes blocked.

DOCKER:
    foreground = main container process (PID 1)

Example:
    PID 1 = apache2 -DFOREGROUND

No shell exists initially.

When running:
    docker exec -it container bash

Docker creates ANOTHER process inside container.

Now:
    PID 1 = apache2
    PID 57 = bash

Your shell is NOT PID 1.

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
5. Why apache2 -DFOREGROUND works
============================================================

Apache normally daemonizes itself:
    starts
    detaches
    runs in background

Bad for Docker because:
    PID 1 exits
    container stops

So containers use:
    apache2 -DFOREGROUND

Meaning:
    "stay in foreground"

Now:
    PID 1 = apache2

Container lives as long as Apache lives.

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
6. Why jumpbox container is different
============================================================

Jumpbox behaves like mini VM:
    sshd
    cron
    maybe more services

Docker prefers:
    ONE main process

Problem:
    which service should become PID 1?

Because multiple services exist, people use:

    tail -f /dev/null

to keep container alive after starting services.

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
7. Better alternatives to tail -f /dev/null
============================================================

OPTION 1:
    cron
    exec sshd -D

Result:
    PID 1 = sshd
    cron runs in background

OPTION 2:
    use supervisor

Example:
    supervisord
    s6
    runit

Result:
    PID 1 = supervisor
        -> sshd
        -> cron

OPTION 3:
    full systemd container
    (heavy and uncommon)

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
8. What happens if foreground service crashes
============================================================

Example:
    PID 1 = apache2 -DFOREGROUND

If apache crashes:
    PID 1 exits
    container exits immediately

No internal restart occurs because:
    there is no systemd/supervisor.

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
9. How Docker auto-restart works
============================================================

Docker restarts CONTAINERS, not services.

Example:
    docker run --restart=always ...

Flow:
    apache crashes
    -> container exits
    -> Docker daemon detects exit
    -> Docker starts new container

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
10. Why tail -f /dev/null can be dangerous
============================================================

Example:
    PID 1 = tail

If sshd crashes:
    container STILL appears running

Because:
    PID 1 is still alive

Docker thinks container is healthy even though service is dead.

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
11. Three common container models
============================================================

A) Pure Docker Model
--------------------
PID 1 = app

app crashes
-> container exits
-> Docker restarts container

Most common.

--------------------

B) Fake Keepalive Model
-----------------------
PID 1 = tail

services run in background

service crashes
-> container still running

Simple but unreliable.

--------------------

C) Supervisor Model
-------------------
PID 1 = supervisord

service crashes
-> supervisor restarts service

Closer to VM behavior.

------------------------------------------------------------

============================================================
12. Key Docker philosophy
============================================================

Traditional VM:
    systemd keeps services alive

Docker:
    Docker daemon keeps containers alive

Container = restartable unit

Not individual processes.

